     "result_text_tail": "the runway rule if new contracts cover the hires. I made hiring wait on signed contracts in the recommendation and the next steps.\n\n**Choices I made where I would have asked you:**\n- **Audience and look:** the board deciding to approve or reject; the navy theme.\n- **The $9.5M cash need:** I read it as covering all company spending, the most generous reading. If it doesn't, the bank empties before month 18. Runway is measured at today's burn, which is also generous.\n- **Contribution per order:** I count it after site fixed costs but before customer acquisition and head-office costs. Break-even baskets keep every other median input fixed.\n- **Zipcart case:** applying Zipcart's fee and promotion cut to a median site is my estimate. It assumes the same 14% drop in orders.\n- **Nine-store annual figures:** I scaled per-order results up to a full year at mature volume, every day, and say so on the slide.\n- **Tests before a return:** for example, a customer site making money per order for two straight quarters. These are my recommendation, not figures from the briefing pack, and the slide says so.\n\n**Formatting limit:** the renderer shows per-order dollars to one decimal outside charts (e.g. \"\u2212$1.9\"). On the slide comparing cases, I set the bars to one decimal so they match the calculation cards under them.\n\nNext, I can switch the theme or rework any section.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/` (the spec and its builder script are in `./work/`):\n- parcelbird.pptx\n- parcelbird-sources.xlsx"
    }
